* set.access
*
* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - put the new account's Windows user into the groups
* its remote access calls for.  access.ssh and access.api come from the SSH /
* API / BOTH / NONE keyword in more.args, or from the ADMINISTRATOR tier, which
* forces both.
*
* USER ACCOUNTS ONLY.  A GROUP account has no Windows user - the GROUP arm
* reaches neither create_user() nor set_passwd() - and is reached with LOGTO or
* an F pointer, so there is nothing here for it and it never calls this.
*
* NO "already a member" TEST, unlike MODIFY.ACCOUNT's route.set.  The Windows
* user was created moments ago in this same verb, so it is in no groups but the
* ones already added above; asking would be asking a question whose answer is
* known.  The ADOPT path is the one exception and it is deliberate that it
* joins too - owner's rule of 21 Aug 2026 is that ALL administrators have both
* routes, and an adopted account is always ADMINISTRATOR (see more.args).
*
* A FAILURE STOPS THE VERB, matching every other group step here.  An account
* whose access is not what was asked for is worse than no account: nobody would
* re-check, and the failure would surface later as "the client cannot connect".
set.access:
   if access.ssh then
*     PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 94 - take the answer; see the sdusers site above.
      stat = os_group("ADDMEM", "sdssh", acc.uname)
      if stat # 0 then
         @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
         stop sysmsg(10081,acc.uname,stat) ;* Unable to change remote access for %1
      end
   end

   if access.api then
*     PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 94 - take the answer; see the sdusers site above.
      stat = os_group("ADDMEM", "sdapi", acc.uname)
      if stat # 0 then
         @system.return.code = -ER$FAILED
         stop sysmsg(10081,acc.uname,stat) ;* Unable to change remote access for %1
      end
   end

*  Says the whole resulting state, in the same four messages MODIFY.ACCOUNT
*  uses, so an account's access reads identically whether it was just made or
*  just changed.
* 05 Sep 26 Windows port - ***AN ADMINISTRATOR'S ROUTES ARE LOCAL ONLY, AND
*   SAYING "ssh and the API" WAS FALSE FROM THE MOMENT 167 LANDED.***
*   PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 169 (a).  Found in verify-sshadmin's own PASSING
*   transcript on b120: this verb printed "SD routes for SDSADMB120A: ssh and
*   the API." and four seconds later LOGIN wrote "LOGIN REFUSED account=SDSYS
*   reason=administrator...".  The same run said both.
*
*   ***THE GRANT IS NOT WRONG - THE SENTENCE WAS.***  :1718 forces access.ssh
*   and access.api true for this tier and that is still right after the owner's
*   refinement of 5 Sep: an administrator DOES use ssh and the API, from this
*   machine.  What they cannot do is reach them from another one, which is
*   LOGIN's peer test.  So the fix is a truthful message, not a narrower grant,
*   and MODIFY.ACCOUNT's refusal to remove the two (10083) keeps its reason.
*
* 18 Sep 26 Windows port - RELEASE_1.1 64.  ***THE TIER CASE STOOD FIRST HERE AND
*   IS DELETED, with the tier whose routes it reported.***  It printed 10175
*   ("no routes; the console is the only door"), a message slice 4 deleted along
*   with the administrator route model - so what stood here was unreachable code
*   naming a message file that no longer exists.  What is left is the three
*   general cases and the fallback, which is the whole of what an account's
*   routes can be now: per-account, chosen when the account is made, with no
*   rule anywhere that overrides them.
   begin case
      case access.ssh and access.api
         crt sysmsg(10078,acc.uname) ;* may sign in over ssh and use the API
      case access.ssh
         crt sysmsg(10076,acc.uname) ;* ssh, and may not use the API
      case access.api
         crt sysmsg(10077,acc.uname) ;* the API, and may not sign in over ssh
      case 1
         crt sysmsg(10079,acc.uname) ;* no remote access at all
   end case

*  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES 125: warn (non-prohibitively) when the access just granted
*  has no active transport on this machine.  The grant is a group membership
*  that persists and takes effect when the transport comes on, so this is a
*  heads-up for an account provisioned ahead of its transport, NOT a refusal -
*  the owner ruled that provisioning first is a legitimate workflow.  Native
*  checks only (sshd.exe on disk; sd.conf's APIPORT for the listener), so
*  create.account gains no PowerShell probe on its hot path.
   if access.ssh and not(ospath('C:\WINDOWS\System32\OpenSSH\sshd.exe', OS$EXISTS)) then
      crt sysmsg(10161, acc.uname)  ;* set up for ssh, but no ssh server yet
   end
   if access.api and (config('APIPORT') + 0) <= 0 then
      crt sysmsg(10162, acc.uname)  ;* set up for the API, but no listener yet
   end
return

